// Benchmark questions. One JSON object per line, schema in src/legalgraph/benchmark/schema.py
// Validate with: python scripts/validate_benchmark.py
// These five are worked examples, verified against the clause text. Keep the format
// and append your own. Target ~200.
{"id": "CA13-001", "question": "Someone applies for a company's shares under a fictitious name. What punishment do they face?", "answer": "They are liable for action for fraud: imprisonment of six months to ten years, plus a fine of not less than the amount involved in the fraud and up to three times that amount. Where the fraud involves public interest the minimum term is higher.", "gold_clauses": ["s38(1)", "s447"], "anchor_clause": "s38(1)", "reference_path": [["s38(1)", "s447"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s38(1) names the offence but states no penalty, only 'liable for action under section 447'. The sentence itself is in s447, whose heading is 'Punishment for fraud' and which shares almost no vocabulary with the question."}
{"id": "CA13-002", "question": "By when must a company file its financial statements with the Registrar before the daily fine starts running?", "answer": "Within the time specified in section 403, which allows filing within the period set by the relevant provision, and late filing within a further 270 days on payment of additional fee. Past that, the company is fined one thousand rupees per day, capped at ten lakh rupees.", "gold_clauses": ["s137(3)", "s403"], "anchor_clause": "s137(3)", "reference_path": [["s137(3)", "s403"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "The penalty is in s137(3) but the deadline it hinges on is only in s403. Asking about the deadline retrieves s137(3), which does not contain it."}
{"id": "CA13-003", "question": "Does the Board need a special resolution from shareholders to contribute company funds to the National Defence Fund?", "answer": "No. Section 183(1) lets the Board contribute any amount it thinks fit notwithstanding sections 180, 181 and 182, so the special-resolution requirement that would otherwise apply under section 180 is displaced.", "gold_clauses": ["s183(1)", "s180"], "anchor_clause": "s183(1)", "reference_path": [["s183(1)", "s180"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Good discriminating case: reading s180 alone gives the opposite answer. The 'notwithstanding' override is what makes the reference consequential."}
{"id": "CA13-004", "question": "Can an independent director be granted stock options as part of their remuneration?", "answer": "No. Under section 149(9) an independent director is not entitled to any stock option, and may receive only sitting fees, reimbursement of expenses and profit-related commission, subject to the limits in sections 197 and 198.", "gold_clauses": ["s149(9)", "s197", "s198"], "anchor_clause": "s149(9)", "reference_path": [["s149(9)", "s197"], ["s149(9)", "s198"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Three gold clauses from one anchor. The prohibition is answerable from s149(9) alone, but the permitted remuneration is bounded by s197 and computed under s198."}
{"id": "CA13-005", "question": "A company gives false information in its incorporation application. What are the consequences for the promoters who signed it?", "answer": "The promoters, the persons named as first directors and those making the declaration are liable for action for fraud under section 447, which carries imprisonment of six months to ten years and a fine of not less than the amount involved, up to three times that amount.", "gold_clauses": ["s7(5)", "s7(6)", "s447"], "anchor_clause": "s7(5)", "reference_path": [["s7(5)", "s447"], ["s7(6)", "s447"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Two anchor subsections converge on the same target. s447 is the most-referenced clause in the Act (in-degree 14) and is unreachable from the question wording by semantic similarity."}
{"id": "CA13-006", "question": "A company wants to issue shares to its employees for less than market value. Is that allowed?", "answer": "Not as a discounted issue: section 53 prohibits issuing shares at a discount and any such share is void, with fines up to five lakh rupees. But section 54 permits sweat equity shares notwithstanding section 53, if the issue is authorised by special resolution specifying the number, price, consideration and class of recipients, and at least one year has passed since the company commenced business.", "gold_clauses": ["s54(1)", "s53"], "anchor_clause": "s54(1)", "reference_path": [["s54(1)", "s53"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Strong discriminator: s53 alone answers 'no', s54 reverses it. The 'notwithstanding' override is the whole point."}
{"id": "CA13-007", "question": "In a winding up, who gets paid first, government taxes owed by the company or the workmen's dues?", "answer": "Workmen's dues. Section 327(1) lists revenues and taxes as preferential payments, but it is expressly subject to section 326, under which workmen's dues and secured creditors' debts ranking pari passu with them are paid in priority to all other debts notwithstanding anything else in the Act.", "gold_clauses": ["s327(1)", "s326"], "anchor_clause": "s327(1)", "reference_path": [["s327(1)", "s326"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Reading s327 alone gives the wrong priority order. Classic practitioner question."}
{"id": "CA13-008", "question": "Who actually sets the accounting standards that a capital reduction has to comply with?", "answer": "The Central Government prescribes them, as recommended by the Institute of Chartered Accountants of India and after examining the recommendations of the National Financial Reporting Authority. Section 66(3) bars the Tribunal from sanctioning a reduction unless the accounting treatment conforms to those standards specified in section 133.", "gold_clauses": ["s66(3)", "s133"], "anchor_clause": "s66(3)", "reference_path": [["s66(3)", "s133"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s66(3) names the requirement; only s133 says who sets the standards and through what process."}
{"id": "CA13-009", "question": "What is the ceiling on total managerial remuneration a public company can pay when it appoints a managing director?", "answer": "Eleven per cent of net profits for the financial year, computed under section 198. Section 196(4) makes the appointment and its remuneration subject to section 197 and Schedule V, and section 197(1) sets the eleven per cent limit, which can be exceeded only in general meeting with Central Government approval.", "gold_clauses": ["s196(4)", "s197"], "anchor_clause": "s196(4)", "reference_path": [["s196(4)", "s197"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "The number is only in s197. s196(4) just points at it."}
{"id": "CA13-010", "question": "A company has not filed its annual return. Can it still buy back its own shares?", "answer": "No. Section 70(2) bars a company from purchasing its own shares, directly or indirectly, where it has not complied with sections 92, 123, 127 or 129. Section 92 is the obligation to prepare and file the annual return.", "gold_clauses": ["s70(2)", "s92"], "anchor_clause": "s70(2)", "reference_path": [["s70(2)", "s92"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s70(2) cites four section numbers with no description, so the question is unanswerable without resolving at least one of them."}
{"id": "CA13-011", "question": "A company shifted its registered office to another State two months ago. Can it now apply to have its name struck off the register?", "answer": "No. Section 249(1)(a) bars an application under section 248(2) if the company has changed its name or shifted its registered office from one State to another at any time in the previous three months. The strike-off route itself is section 248(2), which requires extinguishing all liabilities and a special resolution or consent of seventy-five per cent of members by paid-up capital.", "gold_clauses": ["s249(1)", "s248(2)"], "anchor_clause": "s249(1)", "reference_path": [["s249(1)", "s248(2)"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Answerable from s249(1) alone for the bar, but the procedure being barred is only described in s248(2)."}
{"id": "CA13-012", "question": "What is the penalty if a company fails to appoint an auditor at its first annual general meeting?", "answer": "A fine of not less than twenty-five thousand and up to five lakh rupees for the company, and for every officer in default imprisonment up to one year or a fine of ten thousand to one lakh rupees, or both. The appointment duty is in section 139(1); the penalty for contravening sections 139 to 146 is in section 147(1).", "gold_clauses": ["s147(1)", "s139"], "anchor_clause": "s139", "reference_path": [["s147(1)", "s139"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Reverse direction: the anchor a retriever finds from the question is s139, and s147(1) is the clause that references it. Tests whether expansion follows incoming edges too, not just outgoing."}
{"id": "CA13-013", "question": "Can a company's auditor also keep its books of account?", "answer": "No. Section 144 bars an auditor from providing accounting and book-keeping services to the company, its holding company or its subsidiary. Contravention is punishable under section 147(1) with a fine of twenty-five thousand to five lakh rupees for the company, and imprisonment up to one year or a fine for every officer in default.", "gold_clauses": ["s144", "s147(1)"], "anchor_clause": "s144", "reference_path": [["s147(1)", "s144"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Same incoming-edge shape as CA13-012. The prohibition and its consequence sit in different sections."}
{"id": "CA13-014", "question": "A scheme of arrangement before the Tribunal includes a buy-back of the company's shares. What extra conditions apply to the buy-back?", "answer": "It must comply with section 68, so the buy-back has to be funded from free reserves, the securities premium account or the proceeds of a fresh issue, and not from the proceeds of an earlier issue of the same kind of securities, along with the conditions in section 68(2). Section 230(10) bars the Tribunal from sanctioning the compromise otherwise.", "gold_clauses": ["s230(10)", "s68"], "anchor_clause": "s230(10)", "reference_path": [["s230(10)", "s68"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s230(10) states the requirement in one line and contains none of the substance."}
{"id": "CA13-015", "question": "A public company wants to accept fixed deposits from the general public rather than only its members. Is that permitted?", "answer": "Yes, within limits. Section 73 prohibits companies from inviting or accepting deposits from the public, but section 76(1) allows a public company with the prescribed net worth or turnover to do so notwithstanding section 73, provided it complies with section 73(2) and obtains a credit rating from a recognised agency.", "gold_clauses": ["s76(1)", "s73"], "anchor_clause": "s76(1)", "reference_path": [["s76(1)", "s73"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Another override pair where s73 alone gives the wrong answer."}
{"id": "CA13-016", "question": "A company has outstanding unpaid deposits from before the Act commenced. Can it declare a dividend on its equity shares?", "answer": "No, not while the failure continues. Section 123(6) bars a company that has failed to comply with sections 73 and 74 from declaring any dividend on its equity shares. Section 74 requires such a company to file a statement with the Registrar within three months and repay the deposits.", "gold_clauses": ["s123(6)", "s74"], "anchor_clause": "s123(6)", "reference_path": [["s123(6)", "s74"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s123(6) cites the section numbers only; what compliance means is in s74."}
{"id": "CA13-017", "question": "Someone was convicted of a related party transaction offence three years ago. Can they be appointed a director?", "answer": "No. Section 164(1)(g) disqualifies a person convicted of the offence dealing with related party transactions under section 188 at any time in the preceding five years. Section 188 is the provision requiring Board consent for contracts with related parties covering sale or purchase of goods, property, leasing and similar arrangements.", "gold_clauses": ["s164(1)(g)", "s188"], "anchor_clause": "s164(1)(g)", "reference_path": [["s164(1)(g)", "s188"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "The disqualification is readable from s164(1)(g), but identifying which conduct counts needs s188."}
{"id": "CA13-018", "question": "What must the Board's report say about the company's Audit Committee?", "answer": "Under section 177(8) the Board's report must disclose the composition of the Audit Committee, and where the Board did not accept any recommendation of the Committee, that fact with reasons. The report itself is the one required by section 134(3), which lists everything else it must contain.", "gold_clauses": ["s177(8)", "s134(3)"], "anchor_clause": "s177(8)", "reference_path": [["s177(8)", "s134(3)"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s134(3) has in-degree 7, a hub for disclosure obligations scattered across the Act."}
{"id": "CA13-019", "question": "Can a company be registered with a name suggesting it has Central Government backing?", "answer": "No. Section 4(3) bars registration with a name containing any word or expression likely to give the impression that the company is connected with or has the patronage of the Central Government, a State Government or any local authority or government body. A later change of name is made subject to the same restriction by section 13(2).", "gold_clauses": ["s13(2)", "s4(3)"], "anchor_clause": "s13(2)", "reference_path": [["s13(2)", "s4(3)"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Pairs with CA13-020: the same anchor points at two different restrictions."}
{"id": "CA13-020", "question": "A company wants to change its name to one very similar to an existing registered company. Will that be approved?", "answer": "No. Section 4(2) bars a name that is identical with or too nearly resembles the name of an existing company registered under the Act or any previous company law, and section 13(2) makes a change of name subject to section 4(2) and (3), with Central Government approval required in writing.", "gold_clauses": ["s13(2)", "s4(2)"], "anchor_clause": "s13(2)", "reference_path": [["s13(2)", "s4(2)"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Same anchor as CA13-019, different target. Keeps both or drops both; do not keep one."}
{"id": "CA13-021", "question": "Do all equity shareholders get one vote per share?", "answer": "Not necessarily. Section 47(1) gives every equity shareholder a right to vote on every resolution, in proportion to their share of paid-up equity capital, but it is subject to section 43, which allows equity share capital with differential rights as to dividend, voting or otherwise in accordance with prescribed rules.", "gold_clauses": ["s47(1)", "s43"], "anchor_clause": "s47(1)", "reference_path": [["s47(1)", "s43"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s47(1) read alone implies strict proportionality; s43 is what permits differential voting rights."}
{"id": "CA13-022", "question": "The Serious Fraud Investigation Office is investigating a company. Can the company suspend an employee during the investigation?", "answer": "Not without Tribunal approval. Section 218 protects employees during an investigation of a company's affairs under section 210, 212, 213 or 219. Section 212 is the SFIO investigation, assigned by the Central Government on a Registrar's or inspector's report, on a special resolution, in the public interest, or on a government department's request.", "gold_clauses": ["s218(1)(a)", "s212"], "anchor_clause": "s218(1)(a)", "reference_path": [["s218(1)(a)", "s212"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s218(1)(a) lists four investigation sections by number with no description of any."}
{"id": "CA13-023", "question": "When can the Central Government petition to wind up a company after an inspector's report?", "answer": "Where the company is liable to be wound up and the report under section 223 shows circumstances of the kind referred to in section 213, which covers affairs conducted with intent to defraud creditors or members, unlawful purposes, or members not being given the information they are entitled to. Section 224(2) then lets the Central Government present a winding-up petition on just and equitable grounds.", "gold_clauses": ["s224(2)", "s213"], "anchor_clause": "s224(2)", "reference_path": [["s224(2)", "s213"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "The grounds are entirely in s213. VERIFY the s213 summary against the full clause text before keeping."}
{"id": "CA13-024", "question": "A foreign company issues a prospectus in India containing a knowingly misleading profit forecast. Is that an offence under the Companies Act?", "answer": "Yes. Section 391(1) applies sections 34 to 36 to a prospectus issued by a company incorporated outside India under section 389, and section 36 punishes any person who knowingly or recklessly makes a false, deceptive or misleading statement, promise or forecast, or deliberately conceals material facts, to induce another to acquire or subscribe for securities.", "gold_clauses": ["s391(1)", "s36"], "anchor_clause": "s391(1)", "reference_path": [["s391(1)", "s36"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "s391(1) is pure cross-reference machinery; all substance is in s36."}
{"id": "CA13-025", "question": "A private placement offer is being made. Do the prospectus content rules still apply?", "answer": "Section 42(1) permits private placement through a private placement offer letter without prejudice to section 26, so section 26's requirements for matters to be stated in a prospectus are not displaced. Section 26 requires the prospectus to be dated, signed, and to state registered office and officer details, issue opening and closing dates, and the other prescribed particulars.", "gold_clauses": ["s42(1)", "s26"], "anchor_clause": "s42(1)", "reference_path": [["s42(1)", "s26"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "'Without prejudice to' is a weaker trigger than 'notwithstanding'. Worth checking whether the question is too lawyerly to be natural."}
{"id": "CA13-026", "question": "A company has been declared sick by the Tribunal. Who can apply for its revival?", "answer": "Any secured creditor of that company, or the company itself, may apply to the Tribunal for determination of revival and rehabilitation measures. The determination of sickness is made under section 253, which is triggered where secured creditors representing fifty per cent or more of outstanding debt have demanded payment and the company has failed to pay, secure or compound it within thirty days of the notice of demand.", "gold_clauses": ["s254(1)", "s253"], "anchor_clause": "s254(1)", "reference_path": [["s254(1)", "s253"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "The sickness threshold is only in s253. Note this provision was later omitted by the IBC; the 2017-03-28 expression still contains it, which is itself worth a sentence in the paper."}
{"id": "CA13-027", "question": "The fast-track merger route is being used. Can it also cover a scheme of compromise with creditors?", "answer": "Yes. Section 233(12) applies the fast-track provisions mutatis mutandis to a scheme of compromise or arrangement referred to in section 230, under which the Tribunal may order meetings of creditors or members to consider the arrangement on an application by the company, a creditor, a member or a liquidator.", "gold_clauses": ["s233(12)", "s230"], "anchor_clause": "s233(12)", "reference_path": [["s233(12)", "s230"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Procedural rather than substantive. Lower value than the override pairs; drop if trimming."}
{"id": "CA13-028", "question": "The Central Government objects to a fast-track merger scheme. What happens next?", "answer": "Within sixty days of receiving the scheme the Central Government may apply to the Tribunal stating its objections and ask that the scheme be considered under section 232, which is the full merger and amalgamation procedure involving reconstruction, transfer of undertaking, property or liabilities between transferor and transferee companies.", "gold_clauses": ["s233(5)", "s232"], "anchor_clause": "s233(5)", "reference_path": [["s233(5)", "s232"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "The consequence of the objection is only legible via s232."}
{"id": "CA13-029", "question": "Can a company that has not complied with the financial statement rules buy back its shares?", "answer": "No. Section 70(2) bars a buy-back where the company has not complied with sections 92, 123, 127 or 129. Section 129 requires financial statements to give a true and fair view, comply with the accounting standards notified under section 133, and follow the forms in Schedule III.", "gold_clauses": ["s70(2)", "s129"], "anchor_clause": "s70(2)", "reference_path": [["s70(2)", "s129"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Same anchor as CA13-010, different target of the same four-section list. Keep both or neither."}
{"id": "CA13-030", "question": "How long can an independent director serve, and how are they appointed?", "answer": "Up to five consecutive years under section 149(10), with reappointment possible on a special resolution and disclosure in the Board's report. The appointment itself is subject to section 152, under which every director is appointed by the company in general meeting save as otherwise expressly provided.", "gold_clauses": ["s149(10)", "s152"], "anchor_clause": "s149(10)", "reference_path": [["s149(10)", "s152"]], "difficulty": "one_hop", "annotator": "vm", "notes": "Two-part question, which may be unnatural. The s152 link is weaker than most here; candidate for rejection."}